            <ArticleTitle>Propionibacterium Acnes Infection of a Metacarpophalangeal Joint Arthroplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>394.e1-394.e6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0363-5023(16)30985-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jhsa.2017.01.023</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Neglected and underestimated in the past, Propionibacterium acnes is currently the most prevalent organism associated with deep prosthetic infections around the shoulder. Surprisingly, it has never been reported as a cause of infection in the hand. Here we report a case of a late presentation of a P. acnes infection in a metacarpophalangeal joint replacement, resulting in chronic low-grade pain with movement. The patient underwent a 2-stage revision, with initial removal of the prosthesis. Positive cultures for P. acnes required 15 days of extended incubation. The patient subsequently had 6 weeks of oral antibiotics followed by a second-stage revision with a Silastic implant.</AbstractText>
